WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.158
Look at these two lines.

2
00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.020
Both have one pair of round brackets

3
00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.710
and one pair of square brackets.

4
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.270
The first is fine.

5
00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.860
The second is a syntax error.

6
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.545
Every compiler and every editor

7
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.480
has to tell them apart.

8
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.210
What is the simplest procedure that does it?

9
00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.092
Two classic uses of the structures

10
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.310
from this chapter.

11
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.610
First, matching brackets with a stack.

12
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.928
Then, visiting a network level

13
00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.190
by level with a queue

14
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:32.340
which is a preview of Chapter nine.

15
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.160
A first idea is to count.

16
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.152
Does every opening bracket have a

17
00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.320
closing bracket of its own kind?

18
00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.240
The wrong line passes that test

19
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.900
one round pair, one square pair.

20
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.140
But the brackets cross each other

21
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.000
and that is not allowed.

22
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.910
Brackets must nest, like boxes inside boxes.

23
00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.285
The rule is

24
00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.447
the bracket you close must be the

25
00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.650
one you opened most recently.

26
00:00:58.650 --> 00:00:59.750
Most recent.

27
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.100
That should ring a bell.

28
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.770
Read the text from left to right.

29
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.770
When you see an opening bracket

30
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.410
push it on a stack.

31
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.470
When you see a closing bracket

32
00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.290
pop the stack

33
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.290
and check that the two are a pair.

34
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.120
Other characters are ignored.

35
00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.800
Here, the square bracket is pushed

36
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.035
then the round one.

37
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.869
The round closing bracket

38
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.420
pops the round opener: a match.

39
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.925
The square closing bracket

40
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.310
pops the square opener: a match.

41
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.450
At the end the stack must be empty.

42
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:30.770
If something is left

43
00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.140
there is an opener that was never closed.

44
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.730
There are three ways to fail.

45
00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.657
In the first, the closing round bracket

46
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.390
pops a square opener

47
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.910
which is not its partner.

48
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.640
That is a mismatch.

49
00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.045
In the second

50
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.501
the text ends while one opener

51
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:48.940
is still on the stack.

52
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.110
Not closed.

53
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.219
In the third, a closing bracket arrives

54
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:54.750
and the stack is empty.

55
00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.270
There was nothing to match.

56
00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.020
All three are caught by the same loop.

57
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.130
Now a different problem.

58
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.850
Here is a small network.

59
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.590
From A you can go directly to B and to C.

60
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.050
From B you reach D and E

61
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.560
and from C you reach F.

62
00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.900
We want to visit every node, nearest first

63
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.140
first A, then everything one step away

64
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.000
then everything two steps away, and so on.

65
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.260
That is called level order

66
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.230
or breadth-first search.

67
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.840
Put A in a queue.

68
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.140
Now repeat

69
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.880
take the front node out of the queue

70
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:33.716
visit it

71
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.041
and put each of its neighbours that we

72
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.570
haven't seen before at the back.

73
00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.010
Visit A: B and C join the queue.

74
00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.890
Visit B: D and E join, behind C.

75
00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.440
Visit C: F joins.

76
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.060
Visit D, E, F.

77
00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.840
F is also a neighbour of E

78
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.340
but we have already seen it

79
00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.260
so it doesn't join the queue a second time.

80
00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.190
That is what the seen set is for.

81
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:05.322
The visiting order is A, B, C, D, E, F

82
00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.685
all the nodes one step away before

83
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.940
any node two steps away.

84
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.070
Why does a queue give the right order?

85
00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.930
Because it is first in, first out.

86
00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.710
Nodes found earlier are explored earlier

87
00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.240
and nodes found earlier are nearer.

88
00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.374
Swap the queue for a stack

89
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.577
and the same loop dives deep along

90
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.700
one path before coming back.

91
00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.480
That's depth-first search.

92
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.310
We'll compare the two in Chapter nine.

93
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.580
That's the end of Chapter three.

94
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.920
A stack gives back the most recent item.

95
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.720
A queue gives back the oldest.

96
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.490
A double-ended queue gives you both ends.

97
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.268
In practice, you choose by asking which

98
00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.090
item you want next.

99
00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.190
Most recent: stack.

100
00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:50.990
Oldest: queue.

101
00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:53.840
Either end: a double-ended queue.

102
00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.740
In the next chapter we'll see recursion

103
00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.670
which uses a stack you never had to write.

104
00:04:00.297 --> 00:04:02.617
Both applications, in Python.

105
00:04:03.197 --> 00:04:05.060
Here is the bracket checker.

106
00:04:05.127 --> 00:04:07.105
It uses the array stack we built

107
00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:08.857
earlier in this chapter.

108
00:04:08.927 --> 00:04:10.605
Openers and closers are two

109
00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.167
strings in the same order

110
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:15.317
so the index of a bracket tells us its kind.

111
00:04:15.667 --> 00:04:17.757
For an opener, we push.

112
00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:18.857
For a closer

113
00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:21.417
we first check that the stack isn't empty

114
00:04:21.417 --> 00:04:23.575
and then pop and compare the kinds

115
00:04:23.637 --> 00:04:26.204
the index in closers must equal the

116
00:04:26.277 --> 00:04:27.937
index of the popped opener.

117
00:04:27.937 --> 00:04:30.867
At the end, the stack must be empty.

118
00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:33.677
Running it: the first expression

119
00:04:33.677 --> 00:04:36.397
with properly nested brackets, returns True.

120
00:04:36.397 --> 00:04:39.237
The crossed brackets return False.

121
00:04:39.237 --> 00:04:41.897
The unclosed one, with an extra opener

122
00:04:41.897 --> 00:04:42.657
returns False.

123
00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:45.447
And the extra closer returns False.

124
00:04:46.027 --> 00:04:47.777
Now level order.

125
00:04:47.777 --> 00:04:49.513
The graph is a dictionary

126
00:04:49.577 --> 00:04:51.712
each key is a node and each value

127
00:04:51.777 --> 00:04:53.257
is the list of its neighbours.

128
00:04:53.257 --> 00:04:54.857
We use our array queue

129
00:04:54.857 --> 00:04:56.637
and a set called seen

130
00:04:56.637 --> 00:04:58.787
which starts with the start node.

131
00:04:59.137 --> 00:05:00.647
Each time through the loop

132
00:05:00.647 --> 00:05:02.367
we dequeue a node

133
00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:04.223
append it to the order list

134
00:05:04.287 --> 00:05:06.061
and enqueue every neighbour

135
00:05:06.127 --> 00:05:08.107
that isn't in seen.

136
00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:10.547
We add it to seen at that moment

137
00:05:10.547 --> 00:05:11.967
when it is enqueued

138
00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:13.852
not when it is dequeued.

139
00:05:13.927 --> 00:05:15.389
That is what stops a node

140
00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:16.977
from being queued twice.

141
00:05:17.557 --> 00:05:20.977
The result is A, B, C, D, E, F.

142
00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:22.728
This second block prints the

143
00:05:22.787 --> 00:05:25.267
queue after each visit: after A

144
00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:27.007
the queue holds B and C.

145
00:05:27.007 --> 00:05:30.227
After B, it holds C, D and E.

146
00:05:30.227 --> 00:05:33.637
After C, D, E and F.

147
00:05:33.987 --> 00:05:36.037
And by the time we visit E

148
00:05:36.037 --> 00:05:38.297
the node F is already waiting

149
00:05:38.297 --> 00:05:40.347
so it is never added again.

150
00:05:40.927 --> 00:05:42.362
How fast are these?

151
00:05:42.437 --> 00:05:44.014
The bracket checker looks

152
00:05:44.077 --> 00:05:45.637
at each character once

153
00:05:45.637 --> 00:05:48.334
and does a constant amount of work for it

154
00:05:48.397 --> 00:05:50.214
so it takes time proportional

155
00:05:50.277 --> 00:05:52.447
to the length of the text.

156
00:05:52.797 --> 00:05:55.369
Level order enqueues and dequeues

157
00:05:55.447 --> 00:05:56.667
each node once

158
00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:58.539
and looks at each neighbour list once.

159
00:05:58.587 --> 00:06:01.128
So it takes time proportional to the number

160
00:06:01.187 --> 00:06:03.538
of nodes plus the number of edges.

161
00:06:03.607 --> 00:06:05.075
We'll use exactly that count

162
00:06:05.127 --> 00:06:06.437
again in Chapter nine.

163
00:06:07.017 --> 00:06:09.537
In real code you would use a list as

164
00:06:09.607 --> 00:06:11.626
the stack in the bracket checker

165
00:06:11.687 --> 00:06:13.739
and a collections dot deque as

166
00:06:13.807 --> 00:06:16.527
the queue in the search: append to enqueue

167
00:06:16.527 --> 00:06:18.617
popleft to dequeue.

168
00:06:19.197 --> 00:06:20.667
That's Chapter three.

169
00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:22.717
Next, recursion.

